// mgeHost64 — AgX, THE SCENE'S DISPLAY TRANSFORM (FSL ONLY).  tasks/forge-postprocess.md step 3 (S2).
//
// Replaces the static semi-HDR cubic in tonemap.h.fsl, whose `clamp(c, 0, 2.2)` is the recorded
// blocker for the whole colour chain (forge-deferred-lightmap.md:343). ⚠ THE FAILURE IT FIXES IS A
// HUE FAILURE, NOT A RANGE ONE, and that is why "raise the clamp" was never the answer: above 2.2
// every channel of the cubic flattens TOGETHER, so a warm highlight at R 0.9 / B 0.3 arrives as
// R 0.999 / B 0.526 — saturation down ~37%, orange toward cream. AgX's defining property is
// hue-stable, graceful desaturation toward white as intensity rises; it is built to not do that.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHAT IT IS, IN THE ORDER THE CODE RUNS IT.
//
//   1. INSET MATRIX. A slight desaturation applied BEFORE the curve. This is the whole mechanism:
//      pulling the primaries inward means a channel that is about to clip has already been mixed
//      with its neighbours, so the ratio between channels degrades smoothly instead of snapping to
//      1:1 at the ceiling. The cubic has no equivalent and cannot be given one — it is per-channel.
//   2. LOG2 OVER THE AgX EV RANGE. -12.47393 .. +4.026069, i.e. 10 stops below middle grey and 6.5
//      above it (log2(0.18) = -2.47393; both bounds are that number offset). 16.5 stops of input
//      range against the cubic's 2.2 linear ceiling, which is ~1.1 stops above white.
//   3. THE 6th-ORDER SIGMOID. A polynomial approximation to AgX's tone curve on the normalised log
//      domain. Monotone on [0,1], with a soft toe and a long shoulder — no clamp, no ceiling.
//   4. THE LOOK (agxLook, below), between the sigmoid and the outset — which is where Blender puts
//      it, and it matters: the CDL is defined on the tone-mapped log-ish values, not on radiance.
//   5. OUTSET MATRIX. The inverse of the inset, undoing step 1's desaturation for everything that
//      did NOT reach the shoulder. What is left is exactly the desaturation the highlights earned.
//   6. pow(·, 2.2), back to LINEAR DISPLAY.
//
// So: LINEAR SCENE RADIANCE IN, LINEAR DISPLAY OUT. That is a different contract from tonemap.h.fsl,
// which was fitted against DISPLAY-REFERRED input (0->0, 1->0.84, 2.2->1.0), and it is why the sRGB
// OETF MOVES to the end of resolve.frag under this curve:
//
//     legacy:   straight -> xE -> linearToSrgb -> tonemap        -> dither -> 8-bit
//     AgX:      straight -> xE -> agx -> agxLook -> linearToSrgb -> dither -> 8-bit
//
// ⚠ AND THE CLASS-2 LIFT DIES WITH IT. sky/statics_add/statics_blend wrote inverseTonemap(authored)
// so the final curve handed MW's value back unchanged. That inverse is the LEGACY curve's; keeping
// it under AgX is not "the sky staying a reference", it is simply broken. scenecolor.h.fsl's
// liftInPass() is disarmed by the same host bit that selects this curve — one expression, two
// receivers (gShadowParams.toneParams.w and gResolveParams.tone.y).
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ⚠ EXPECT AgX **BASE** TO READ FLAT AND DESATURATED, BECAUSE BASE IS WHAT SHIPS. That is not a
// regression, it is the transform working: AgX's base is deliberately low-contrast and the punch
// comes from the LOOK on top. It ships neutral (1/1/1/0) so that the first thing anyone judges is
// the base transform itself — the blocker S2 exists to fix is a HUE failure, and a look stacked on
// top is exactly what makes a hue judgement unattributable. Choose the look FROM here.
//
// ⚠ WHERE 0.18 LANDS, because it is the one number that gates a mistranscription. Middle grey
// (0.18 linear scene) leaves this file at 0.2145 LINEAR DISPLAY, i.e. **0.5005 as an sRGB display
// code** once resolve.frag's linearToSrgb runs — AgX's signature property, middle grey at half
// code. 1.0 linear scene lands at 0.7919. The host asserts both at init (forgerender.cpp's AgX
// self-test) rather than trusting the transcription: every constant below is prior art, and a
// mistyped polynomial coefficient or a transposed matrix row would otherwise present only as "the
// colours look a bit off". See [[feedback_prior_art_constants_dont_transfer]].
//
// ⚠ ONE STATED APPROXIMATION: step 6's pow(·, 2.2) is AgX's "2.2 exponent reference display" EOTF,
// and resolve.frag re-encodes with the PIECEWISE sRGB OETF (linearize.h.fsl explains why the
// piecewise pair is the house transfer function and pow(x, 2.2) appears nowhere else). The two
// disagree only in the sRGB toe, below ~0.03 display, where the round trip lifts the darkest codes
// by under 1/255. Kept rather than "fixed" by swapping in linearToSrgb here, because 2.2 is the
// display AgX's shoulder was FITTED against and re-fitting it is a different piece of work.
//
// SHADER-ONLY, and NO SRT DEPENDENCY — the same split as tonemap.h.fsl / linearize.h.fsl, for the
// same load-bearing reason: resolve.frag has a private SRT, cannot see gShadowParams, and needs the
// maths without the policy. The policy (which pass applies what) stays in scenecolor.h.fsl.

#ifndef AGX_H
#define AGX_H

// The AgX log domain. Both are log2(0.18) offset: -2.47393 - 10 and -2.47393 + 6.5.
#define AGX_MIN_EV (-12.47393f)
#define AGX_MAX_EV (4.026069f)

// ⚠ WRITTEN AS EXPLICIT ROW DOTS, NOT AS A MATRIX LITERAL, and deliberately. Every published copy of
// these numbers is a GLSL `mat3(...)` constructor, which is COLUMN-major — transcribing it into an
// HLSL float3x3 literal (row-major) transposes it silently, and a transposed near-diagonal matrix
// still looks almost right. Rows here are rows.
//
// Row sums are 1.000136 / 0.999963 / 0.999952: grey in is grey out to ~1.8e-4, which is what makes
// the host's neutral-axis mirror exact enough to invert against. The self-test measures that drift
// rather than assuming it.
float3 agxInset(float3 v)
{
    return float3(
        dot(v, float3(0.842479062253094f,  0.0784335999999992f, 0.0792237451477643f)),
        dot(v, float3(0.0423282422610123f, 0.878468636469772f,  0.0791661274605434f)),
        dot(v, float3(0.0423756549057051f, 0.0784336f,          0.879142973793104f)));
}

// The inverse of the above. Row sums 0.999828 / 1.000045 / 1.000059.
float3 agxOutsetMat(float3 v)
{
    return float3(
        dot(v, float3( 1.19687900512017f,   -0.0980208811401368f, -0.0990297440797205f)),
        dot(v, float3(-0.0528968517574562f,  1.15190312990417f,   -0.0989611768448433f)),
        dot(v, float3(-0.0529716355144438f, -0.0980434501171241f,  1.15107367264116f)));
}

// The 6th-order approximation to AgX's default contrast sigmoid, on the normalised [0,1] log domain.
//
// It is an APPROXIMATION and overshoots its endpoints slightly — f(0) = -0.00232, f(1) = 0.99858 —
// which is why every consumer below floors at 0 before a pow(). A negative base under a fractional
// exponent is NaN, and a NaN survives every subsequent multiply, including one by zero coverage
// ([[project_nan_survives_zero_multiply]]). The floor is not defensive tidiness; it is the only
// thing standing between the shoulder's undershoot and a frame of NaNs.
float3 agxContrast(float3 x)
{
    float3 x2 = x * x;
    float3 x4 = x2 * x2;
    return  15.5f    * x4 * x2
          - 40.14f   * x4 * x
          + 31.96f   * x4
          -  6.868f  * x2 * x
          +  0.4298f * x2
          +  0.1191f * x
          -  0.00232f;
}

// Steps 1-3: linear scene radiance -> the AgX tone-mapped log domain, ~[0,1].
float3 agx(float3 v)
{
    v = agxInset(max(v, float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f)));
    // Floor before log2. log2(0) is -inf, and an inf that reaches a multiply is the NaN above by a
    // different road. 1e-10 is log2 = -33.2, i.e. twenty stops below anything AGX_MIN_EV keeps.
    v = clamp(log2(max(v, float3(1.0e-10f, 1.0e-10f, 1.0e-10f))),
              float3(AGX_MIN_EV, AGX_MIN_EV, AGX_MIN_EV),
              float3(AGX_MAX_EV, AGX_MAX_EV, AGX_MAX_EV));
    v = (v - AGX_MIN_EV) * (1.0f / (AGX_MAX_EV - AGX_MIN_EV));
    return agxContrast(v);
}

// Step 4: THE LOOK. ASC CDL — slope/offset/power, then saturation about luma — applied AFTER the
// sigmoid and BEFORE the outset matrix, which is where Blender applies it.
//
// ⚠ SOP FIRST, THEN SAT ON THE SOP OUTPUT (ASC CDL v1.2). The widely-copied GLSL snippet takes
// `luma` from the value BEFORE the CDL, which is invisible at sat = 1 and wrong everywhere else —
// and it would cost the host's neutral-axis mirror its exactness, because desaturating about a
// stale luma MOVES a grey (it stays grey, but it stops equalling the SOP result). Taking luma from
// the SOP output makes saturation identically a no-op on the neutral axis, which is precisely the
// property forgerender.cpp's agxNeutralF() is built on and its self-test gates.
//
// Rec.709 luma weights, not the Rec.601 set rLuminance() uses in resolve.frag. Different jobs: that
// one is a firefly/APL measuring stick and has to agree with the APL instrument; this one is part of
// a colour transform stated in Rec.709 primaries.
//
// AgX BASE is slope 1 / power 1 / sat 1 / offset 0, and that is what SHIPS — an exact identity, so
// the frame's shape is the inset/sigmoid/outset chain and demonstrably not this function. Blender's
// *Punchy* (power 1.20, sat 1.40) is the obvious look if one is wanted; it is not the default,
// because a hue judgement taken with contrast and saturation already stacked on top is not
// attributable to the curve.
float3 agxLook(float3 v, float slope, float power, float sat, float offset)
{
    v = pow(max(v * slope + offset, float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f)),
            float3(power, power, power));
    float luma = dot(v, float3(0.2126f, 0.7152f, 0.0722f));
    return luma + sat * (v - luma);
}

// Steps 5-6: out of the AgX domain and back to LINEAR DISPLAY, ready for resolve.frag's OETF.
float3 agxOutset(float3 v)
{
    v = agxOutsetMat(v);
    return pow(max(v, float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f)), float3(2.2f, 2.2f, 2.2f));
}

#endif // AGX_H
